(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-02"] = {"2.1":{"s":"Elementary transformations are three simple operations on the rows (or columns) of a matrix: swap two rows, multiply a row by a non-zero number, or add a multiple of one row to another. The new matrix is equivalent to the old one, written A ~ B. They are used to find inverses and to solve systems of equations.","f":[{"x":"[A | I] → [I | A⁻¹] using row operations only","n":"Elementary row operation method. Never mix row and column operations in one inverse."}],"w":["Multiply a row only by a NON-ZERO number; multiplying by 0 is not an elementary transformation."],"x":["An elementary transformation is a simple change made to the rows (or the columns) of a matrix. There are three kinds. (1) Interchange two rows: Rᵢ ↔ Rⱼ. (2) Multiply a row by a non-zero number k: Rᵢ → kRᵢ. (3) Add k times one row to another row: Rᵢ → Rᵢ + kRⱼ. The same three can be done on columns, with the letter C in place of R.","After such a change, the new matrix is called equivalent to the old one, and we write A ~ B. Each operation can be undone by another elementary operation, so no information is lost. This is the reason why the operations are used to find inverses and to solve equations.","A good habit is to write the operation next to the row that changes, for example R₂ → R₂ − 2R₁. Then it is easy to check your work, and a teacher can follow your steps.","Common mistake: multiplying a row by zero. It is not an elementary operation, because it destroys the row and it cannot be undone. Another mistake is to change two rows in one step, which gives errors."],"e":"Reduce [[1, 2], [3, 4]] to a triangular form. Apply R₂ → R₂ − 3R₁. Row 2 becomes [3 − 3, 4 − 6] = [0, −2]. The result is [[1, 2], [0, −2]]."},"2.2":{"s":"A square matrix A has an inverse A⁻¹ when AA⁻¹ = A⁻¹A = I; this happens exactly when |A| ≠ 0 (non-singular), and the inverse is unique. It can be found by elementary transformations: apply row operations to A = IA until the left side becomes I, or column operations to A = AI. Or by the adjoint method: A⁻¹ = adj A / |A|, where adj A is the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.","f":[{"x":"A_ij = (-1)^(i + j)*M_ij","n":"Cofactor of element a_ij; M_ij = minor (delete row i, column j).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.46"},{"x":"A^-1 = (1/|A|)*adj(A)","n":"Adjoint method; adj A = transpose of the cofactor matrix; needs |A| != 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.50"},{"x":"A*adj(A) = adj(A)*A = |A|*I","n":"Holds for every square matrix A.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.51"}],"w":["Use only row operations (start from A = IA) or only column operations (A = AI); never mix them.","adj A is the TRANSPOSE of the cofactor matrix; forgetting the transpose is the commonest slip."],"x":["A square matrix A has an inverse A⁻¹ if AA⁻¹ = A⁻¹A = I, the identity matrix. This happens exactly when the determinant |A| is not zero (A is non-singular). If |A| = 0, the matrix is singular and it has no inverse. When the inverse exists, it is unique.","Method 1, the adjoint method: A⁻¹ = adj A/|A|. To find adj A, first find the cofactors A_ij = (−1)^(i+j) M_ij, where M_ij is the minor, that is the determinant that is left when row i and column j are removed. Write them in a matrix, and then take the transpose. This gives adj A. Also A(adj A) = (adj A)A = |A|I. For a 2 × 2 matrix [[a, b], [c, d]], adj A = [[d, −b], [−c, a]].","Method 2, elementary transformations: write A = IA and apply row operations to A until it becomes I. Apply the same operations to the I on the right. When the left becomes I, the right matrix is A⁻¹. You may also use columns with A = AI. Never mix row and column operations in one problem.","Useful facts: (AB)⁻¹ = B⁻¹A⁻¹ (the order is reversed), |A⁻¹| = 1/|A|, (A⁻¹)⁻¹ = A, and for a matrix of order n, |adj A| = |A|^(n−1). Common mistake: forgetting the transpose when finding the adjoint."],"e":"Find the inverse of A = [[2, 1], [5, 3]]. |A| = 6 − 5 = 1. adj A = [[3, −1], [−5, 2]]. So A⁻¹ = [[3, −1], [−5, 2]]. Check: A times A⁻¹ gives [[6 − 5, −2 + 2], [15 − 15, −5 + 6]] = I."},"2.3":{"s":"A system of linear equations can be written as AX = B, with A the coefficient matrix, X the column of unknowns and B the column of constants. Method of inversion: if |A| ≠ 0, X = A⁻¹B. Method of reduction: apply row operations to A (and the same ones to B) until A is triangular, then solve by back substitution.","f":[{"x":"X = A^-1*B","n":"Solution of AX = B by inversion; pre-multiply both sides by A^-1.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.55"}],"w":["Pre-multiply: X = A⁻¹B, not BA⁻¹ (the orders do not even match)."],"x":["A system of linear equations can be written as one matrix equation AX = B. A is the matrix of the coefficients, X is the column of the unknowns, and B is the column of the constants. For example, x + y = 3 and 2x − y = 0 become [[1, 1], [2, −1]] times [x, y] = [3, 0].","Method of inversion: if |A| is not zero, the system has exactly one solution, and X = A⁻¹B. Pre-multiply by A⁻¹, which means writing it on the left. Writing it on the right, BA⁻¹, is wrong, and the orders do not match.","Method of reduction: apply row operations to the augmented matrix [A | B], to get zeros below the diagonal, and then solve from the last equation upwards (back-substitution). This method also works when |A| = 0, and then it tells what is happening. If a row becomes [0 0 0 | non-zero number], the system is inconsistent and has no solution. If a row becomes all zeros, there are infinitely many solutions.","For a homogeneous system AX = O, the solution X = O always exists. A non-zero solution exists only when |A| = 0. Common mistake: thinking that |A| = 0 always means no solution. It can also mean infinitely many solutions."],"e":"Solve 2x + y = 5 and x + y = 3. A = [[2, 1], [1, 1]], |A| = 1, and A⁻¹ = [[1, −1], [−1, 2]]. X = A⁻¹B = [5 − 3, −5 + 6] = [2, 1]. So x = 2 and y = 1. Check: 2(2) + 1 = 5 and 2 + 1 = 3."}};
